{"surah": 114, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ ", "translation": "Say, \"I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَعُوذُ", "translit": "ُhdwُʿعَaʾ", "root": "عوذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِرَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَrِb", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "مَلِكِ ٱلنَّاسِ ", "translation": "The Sovereign of mankind,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَلِكِ", "translit": "ِkِlَm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "إِلَٰهِ ٱلنَّاسِ ", "translation": "The God of mankind,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَٰهِ", "translit": "ِhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "مِن شَرِّ ٱلْوَسْوَاسِ ٱلْخَنَّاسِ ", "translation": "From the evil of the retreating whisperer[1] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "شَرِّ", "translit": "ِّrَhs", "root": "شرر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْوَسْوَاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَwْsَwْlٱ", "root": "وسوس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْخَنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnَhkْlٱ", "root": "خنس"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "ٱلَّذِى يُوَسْوِسُ فِى صُدُورِ ٱلنَّاسِ ", "translation": "Who whispers [evil] into the breasts of mankind -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُوَسْوِسُ", "translit": "ُsِwْsَwُy", "root": "وسوس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "صُدُورِ", "translit": "ِrwُdُṣ", "root": "صدر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "مِنَ ٱلْجِنَّةِ وَٱلنَّاسِ ", "translation": "From among the jinn and mankind.\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْجِنَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّnِjgْlٱ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱَw", "root": "انس"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "قبل طلوع الشمس وقبل انبساطها على الأرض وقبل الغروب سورة الحمد وقل أعوذ برب الناس وقل أعوذ برب الفلق وقل هو الله أحد وقل يا أيها الكافرون وآية الكرسي كل واحدة سبع مرات وتقول سبحان الله والحمد لله ولا إله إلا الله والله أكبر سبعا وتصلي على النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم سبعا وتستغفر لنفسك ولوالديك والمؤمنين وللمؤمنات سبعا وتقول اللهم افعل بي وبهم عاجلا وآجلا في الدين والدنيا والآخرة ما أنت له أهل ولا تفعل بنا يا مولانا ما نحن له أهل إنك غفور حليم جواد كريم رءوف رحيم سبع مرات وانظر أن لا تدع ذلك غدوة وع", "english": "Now, the first watch (al-wird al-awwal) is that which extends from the dawn prayer (ṣalāt al-ṣubḥ) until sunrise (ṭulūʿ al-shams). When the servant has completed his obligatory prayer, he should remain seated in his place of prayer, occupying himself with the remembrance of God most High, until the sun has risen. For it has been reported that the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"That I should sit in my assembly, remembering God most High therein, from the morning prayer until sunrise, is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves.\" {«لأن أقعد في مجلسي أذكر الله تعالى فيه من صلاة الغداة إلى طلوع الشمس أحب إلي من أن أعتق أربع رقاب»} And it is related that he, peace and blessings be upon him, when he had prayed the morning prayer, would sit in his place of prayer until the sun rose; and in some versions [it is added] that he would pray two rakʿahs—that is, after sunrise. Concerning the virtue of this [practice], an incalculable number of reports have come down. Al-Ḥasan related that the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, used to mention, among what he recounted of his Lord's mercy, that He said: \"O son of Adam, remember Me for an hour after the dawn prayer and for an hour after the afternoon prayer, and I shall suffice you for what lies between them.\" {«يا ابن آدم اذكرني بعد صلاة الفجر ساعة وبعد صلاة العصر ساعة أكفك ما بينهما»}", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 260", "section_title_en": "The Daily Liturgy of the Devout Servant: The First Watch (After Dawn Prayer until Sunrise)", "section_index": 260, "anchors": "- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Remembrance, invocation, mention; the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Remembrance of God (dhikr) is the heart's presence with the Divine, ranging from the tongue's utterance to the heart's contemplation; it is the noblest act when accompanied by presence of heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): verbal mention. Metaphorical (majāz): the heart's steadfastness in knowledge of God, which is the reality of dhikr.\n- Root: صلا (ṣallā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Prayer (ṣalāh); root meaning of supplication and blessing; also \"to kindle a fire\" (as in ṣalā al-nār).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣalāh is the servant's standing before God in humility and devotion, comprising praise, recitation, bowing, and prostration; its perfection lies in the heart's presence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the ritual prayer with its pillars. Metaphorical: supplication (duʿā') and blessing (taṣliyah) upon the Prophet.\n- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To arrive at water; a portion or share of something that comes regularly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The daily portion (wird) of devotion—a set liturgy of prayers, recitations, and remembrances that the servant regularly performs at appointed times.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arriving at a watering place. Metaphorical: the soul's regular approach to the fountain of divine grace through prescribed acts of worship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd) — governing the precise enumeration of the four functions (adʿiyah, adhkār, qirā'ah, fikr) after the dawn prayer, each specified by its own governing verb and object.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "هذا أدب القيام فإذا استوى قيامه واستقباله وإطراقه كذلك فليقرأ {قل أعوذ برب الناس} تحصنا به من الشيطان ثم ليأت بالإقامة وإن كان يرجو حضور من يقتدي به فليؤذن أولا ثم ليحضر النية وهو أن ينوي في الظهر مثلا ويقول بقلبه أؤدي فريضة الظهر لله ليميزها بقوله أؤدى عن القضاء وبالفريضة عن النفل وبالظهر عن العصر وغيره ولتكن معاني هذه الألفاظ حاضرة في قلبه فإنه هو النية والألفاظ مذكرات وأسباب لحضورها ويجتهد أن يستديم ذلك إلى آخر التكبير حتى لا يعزب فإذا حضر في قلبه ذلك فليرفع يديه إلى حذو منكبيه بعد إرسالهما بحيث يحاذي بكفيه منكبيه وب", "english": "This is the etiquette of standing. When his standing, his facing, and his lowering of the head are even, let him recite {«قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ»} (Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind), seeking protection thereby from the devil. Then let him perform the iqāmah. And if he hopes for the presence of one who will follow him in prayer, let him give the adhān first. Then let him bring forth the intention (niyyah), which is that he intends, for example, in the ẓuhr prayer, saying in his heart: \"I perform the obligatory ẓuhr prayer for Allah,\" distinguishing it by his utterance: \"I perform it as an obligation (adāʾan)\" from the make-up prayer (qaḍāʾ), and by \"obligatory\" from the supererogatory (nafl), and by \"ẓuhr\" from the ʿaṣr and others. Let the meanings of these words be present in his heart, for that [presence] is the intention itself, while the words are reminders and causes for its presence. Let him strive to sustain this [intention] until the end of the takbīr, so that it does not slip away.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 126", "section_title_en": "Section 126 — The Second Book on the Manner of the Outward Acts of Prayer, Beginning with the Takbīr and What Precedes It", "section_index": 126, "anchors": "- Root: صفن (ṣafana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To draw up one leg and sit upon it; also used of a horse standing on three legs, raising the fourth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The posture of drawing up one foot, as in the description of the swift horses {«الصَّافِنَاتُ الْجِيَادُ»} (the standing steeds, the swift).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical posture of raising one leg. Metaphorical: not applicable here; used strictly in its corporeal sense.\n- Root: صفد (ṣafada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To fetter, bind, or chain together; al-ṣafad is the iron shackle.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Binding in chains, as in {«مُقَرَّنِينَ فِي الْأَصْفَادِ»} (bound together in fetters).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical binding of the feet together. The Prophet (peace be upon him) prohibited both postures in prayer as they mimic the bound or the beast.\n- Root: قصد (qaṣada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To intend, aim, or direct oneself toward something; also moderation and evenness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The intention (niyyah) that directs the heart toward the act, distinguishing one act from another.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to stand upright and face a direction. Metaphorical: the heart's orientation toward the divine command.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic principle that governs the placement of prepositional phrases, here applied to the physical orientation of the body toward the qiblah as a grammatical parallel to the soul's orientation toward Allah.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 170, 658], [2, 658, 1978], [3, 1978, 2810], [4, 2810, 4440]], "114:1": [[1, 170, 658], [2, 658, 1978], [3, 1978, 2810], [4, 2810, 4440]], "2": [[